|[[Angelic Beast Rajakong]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Furious Rajang|Furious Rajang]]
|Similar name, horns, gorilla-like appearance, and attack that produces electricity and [[Wave Attack|waves]]. His lack of tail and resemblance to Ragin' Gory are what suggest Furious Rajang rather than normal [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Rajang|Rajang]].<br>Rajakong also shares his white coloration with [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Blangonga|Blangonga]].
|-
|[[Formicidaean Ariant]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Altaroth|Altaroth]]
|Both are insects that absorb food and resources into their swollen, sac-like abdomens. In addition, Ariant has the second-lowest base HP of all Behemoths, which matches up with how Altaroth is regarded as one of the weakest monsters in the series.
|-
|[[Cumulus Gallus]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:White Fatalis|White Fatalis]]
|Same personality, branching horns, wings, color scheme, lightning-producing capabilities and habitat (a [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Tower|tower]] that extends above the clouds), and both grow stronger after falling below a certain amount of health. Cumulus Gallus' Japanese name, ピヨルーツ ''Piyorūtsu'' ("Piyoroots") combines the onomatopoeia ピヨピヨ ''piyopiyo'' (chirping) with the Japanese name of the White Fatalis Armors, ミラルーツ ''Mirarūtsu'' ("Miraroots"), which is commonly used as a nickname for White Fatalis itself in Japan.
|-
|[[Ancient Magamojoe]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Kulu-Ya-Ku|Kulu-Ya-Ku]]
|His [[:Category:Relic Enemies|Relic]] features resemble Kulu-Ya-Ku's beak, feathers and tail. Fittingly, Kulu-Ya-Ku is a very early-game monster, and Magamojoe debuts in the very first stage of [[Legend Stages# Zero Legends|Zero Legends]].
|-
|[[Great Ape Luza]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Garangolm|Garangolm]]
|Similar concept as a huge, gorilla-like monster with green accents in its design and an association with nature. Though not a Behemoth, [[Zero Luza]] shows further parallels to Garangolm, including heavily armored arms, red eyes when attacking, and becoming more dangerous when sufficiently hurt.
|-
|[[Vermilingua Rangmaster]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Volvidon|Volvidon]]
|Same ears and eye color, similar carapace, and attacks with a long, powerful tongue that crackles with electricity, referencing Volvidon's Tongue Lash and Paralytic Spit attacks. The "Rang" in his name comes from ''Rangurotora'' (ラングロトラ), Volvidon's Japanese name.
|-
|[[Infernal Pegasus Deonil]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Teostra|Teostra]]
|Similar mane, fangs, wings, tail, affinity for fire and Japanese name (テオニール ''Teonīru'', "Teonir"). Its Freeze ability may be based off of the Stun effect that Teostra's Supernova can inflict in the [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Fourth Generation|4th Generation]] of ''Monster Hunter'' games, while its [[Mini-Wave]]s may be a substitute for Teostra's explosions, as Deonil was added before the actual [[Explosion]] ability.
|-
|[[Suidae Piggicus]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Poogie|Poogie]]
|Poogie is a customizable pet pig whose original outfit, the Memorial Stripes, closely resembles the striped fur of Piggicus, a pet pig that thought it was wearing clothes and has a similar Japanese name (ピーギィ ''Pīgyi'', "Piigyi").
|-
|[[Elephantidae Papaou]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Popo|Popo]] and/or [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Elderfrost Gammoth|Elderfrost Gammoth]]
|All three are mammoth-like creatures with tusks and thick fur, and Papaou's name sounds similar to Popo. On the other hand, Papaou's Japanese description says that he aggressively pursues females, and Capcom has [https://twitter.com/CAPCOM_MHB/status/698476971710242816 stated] that all Gammoth found in-game are female, meaning Papaou could represent the idea of an unseen male Gammoth.
|-
|[[Iguanidae Ibilujon]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Deviljho|Deviljho]]
|Same violent and nomadic nature, voracious appetite, level of threat to the ecosystem, green coloration, spikes, tail shape and gaping, drooling mouth. Ibilujon's name is derived from イビルジョー ''Ibirujō'' (Deviljho's Japanese name) annd ''[[Johnnyleon]]'', and its attack animation has a faint cloud of smoke that references Deviljho's signature Dragon Breath attack.
|-
|[[Lophiiformes Angaburu]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Gobul|Gobul]]
|Similar fangs, spikes, lure and purple coloration. Angaburu's name is derived from ''anglerfish'' and チャナガブル ''Chanagaburu'' (Gobul's Japanese name).
|-
|[[Crocodylidae Kurocroc]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Brute Tigrex|Brute Tigrex]]
|Similar Japanese name (クロコックス ''Kurokokkusu'' ("Kurokox" or "Crocox"), winged forelimbs, tail and speed. Their [[:Category:Dark Enemies|Dark]] trait references Brute Tigrex's title (Black Roaring Wyvern), and the red markings on their forelimbs resemble the glowing red veins that become visible through Tigrex's hide when it accelerates its blood circulation.
|-
| rowspan="2"|[[Ensanglion the Calamity]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Shagaru Magala|Shagaru Magala]]
|Ensanglion's Japanese description states that his miasma turns normal beasts into Behemoths, which tend to have vastly increased strength, speed, savagery and aggression compared to their original variants, along with red eyes in the case of the Dark Behemoths and Cumulus Gallus. All of these symptoms are shared with the [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Frenzy Virus|Frenzy Virus]] caused by [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Gore Magala|Gore Magala]] and its adult stage, Shagaru Magala, which acts as the final boss of ''Monster Hunter 4'', just as Ensanglion is the final boss of the [[Behemoth Culling]] maps. Both are also [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Elder Dragon|Elder Dragons]] (Ensanglion is referred to as a 古龍 ''koryū'', "ancient/elder dragon").
|-
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Chaotic Gore Magala|Chaotic Gore Magala]]
|Ensanglion's design has elements of [[Ganglion (Uber Rare Cat)|Ganglion]]'s Normal Form (color) and Evolved Form (wings), which is similar to how Chaotic Gore Magala is stuck halfway between Gore Magala and Shagaru Magala.
|-
|[[Xenobeast Bunaglios]]
|[[Wikia:monsterhunter:Brachydios|Brachydios]] and/or [[Wikia:monsterhunter:Gaismagorm|Gaismagorm]]
|All three are blue, have long tails and can cause [[Explosion]]s. Like Bunaglios, Brachydios has "ios" in its name, is covered in obsidian and punches with its highly developed forearms, while Gaismagorm is implied to have been able to fly until it came to thrive underground by the Archdemon Wingtalon material's description, which says it has "no hint of the divine heights [Gaismagorm] once knew," similar to how Bunaglios was banished to the wilderness and lost the [[:Category:Floating Enemies|Floating]] trait that most [[Teacher Bun Bun|Bun Buns]] have. Gaismagorm is also known as the "Archdemon of the Abyss" and is the final boss of ''Monster Hunter Rise: Sunbreak'', as befits a demonic-looking Great [[:Category:Advent Bosses|Advent Boss]].
|}
*Most Behemoths' Japanese names include a title with the format "Super X Beast" (超X獣 ''Chō Xjū''), where X is a prefix related to the enemy in question. These titles are different for every Behemoth except Ultima Beast Naala and Ancient Magamojoe, who share the same title.
**In English, many Behemoths are given scientific names instead. Strangely, [[Lutrinae Gokurakko]] also has a scientific name despite not being a Behemoth.

